body: "Design a must-compare-and-swap attenuation for every applicable filesystem surface in endojs/endo-but-for-bots, as a design pull request on llm. The maintainer asked for this and asked that a mentat do it, so that every interface is considered.\n\nThe attenuation: given a filesystem capability, make one of the same shape in which every mutation must carry a compare-and-swap precondition, and every direct write is refused. Refused writes include file writes and truncation, directory creation, removal, rename and move, update, delete, attribute and extended-attribute changes, links, and anything else that changes state. A mutation proceeds only when the caller names the state it expects to replace: a content hash, a version or generation, or an explicit \"absent\" for a create. If the precondition does not hold, the mutation fails and changes nothing. Reads are unaffected. Settle the precondition's form, how a caller obtains it from a read, its atomicity guarantee per backend, and how the attenuation composes with the existing ones (read-only, confinement, mount visibility) and with snapshots and trees.\n\nEvery interface must be considered. The design's core is an inventory: a table of every interface and every method that can change state, across JavaScript and Rust and the agent-facing tool schemas. Each row says where the method is defined, whether the attenuation applies, how (the method's CAS form, or why it is refused outright), or why it does not apply. Start from these, and find the rest yourself; the list is not complete:\n- packages/platform/src/fs/interfaces.js: ReadableBlob, SnapshotBlob, ReadableTree, SnapshotTree, PathEntry, PathEntryIssuer, TreeWriter, File, Directory\n- packages/platform/src/fs/extended/type-guards.js: Filesystem, Directory, File, Cursor, OpenFile, Lock, Xattrs, NodeWatcher, BlobRef; and its backends (in-memory, node-fs, from-mount), cached-fs.js, attach.js\n- packages/platform/src/exo-fs.js, fs/checkin.js, fs/checkout.js, fs/confinement.js, fs-node/ (tree-writer, local-tree, local-blob, content-store-powers)\n- packages/daemon/src/interfaces.js: EndoDirectory, EndoMount, EndoMountFile, EndoMountEntry, EndoMountControl, EndoBlob, EndoReadableTree, and the pet-name directory and secret stores where they write; daemon-cas; hashline.js, which may already be a compare-and-swap edit\n- packages/exo-git (EndoGitTree), packages/git, endo-fs-asset-server (AssetMount, AssetPublisher), endo-fs-exec (TreeView), spaces-util (browser-tree)\n- packages/agent-tools json-tools (fs, git-mount, git) and the CLI's file commands\n- any Rust crate under rust/ that exposes or backs a filesystem or tree to JavaScript\nRead the designs already on llm and reconcile with them, citing each: platform-fs, fs-interface-consolidation, fs-interface-reconciliation, endo-fs-backend-seam, daemon-mount, daemon-mount-capabilities, daemon-capability-filesystem, daemon-cas-management, filesystem-watchers, agent-tools-mount-fs-tools, runtime-container-fs-mount, and any others that touch these surfaces.\n\nWrite the design as designs/<name>.md on a branch from current llm, in the repository's design conventions (read designs/README.md and recent designs). It must hold: the semantics, the inventory table, the per-backend atomicity, the composition rules, the errors, a test plan that proves each refused path refuses and each CAS path is atomic, an implementation plan in reviewable steps, and open questions for the maintainers if any remain.\n\nCommit on the branch. In outbox/report.md give the pull request title and description, the inventory's size (interfaces and methods considered, applicable, refused, not applicable), and anything you could not settle. You have no remote: the steward pushes the branch and opens the pull request."
